= 'a@example.org';`\n\n## Scans the query forces\n\n1. **Full table scan on `users`.** `lower(u.email)` wraps the column in a function, so a plain index on `email` can't be used. The engine has to evaluate `lower()` on every row. This is the main problem.\n2. **Scan or large lookup on `orders`.** If `orders.user_id` has no index, the join becomes a full scan of `orders` (hash join or nested loop) instead of an index lookup for the one matching user. With foreign keys, Postgres and SQL Server don't create this index automatically (MySQL/InnoDB does).\n3. **Wide row fetch from `SELECT *`.** This pulls every column from both tables, including any large text or JSON columns. It also duplicates the `user_id`/`id` columns and rules out index-only scans.\n\n## Fixes\n\n**1. Remove the function on the column.** There are two options:\n\n- **Expression index.** This works in Postgres, and in MySQL 8.0.13+ with a functional index. It needs no query change.\n  ```sql\n  CREATE INDEX idx_users_email_lower ON users (lower(email));\n  ```\n  Use `UNIQUE` if emails are unique case-insensitively. The query's `lower(...)` expression must match the index definition exactly.\n- **Case-insensitive type or collation.** Examples are Postgres `citext` and MySQL's default `_ci` collations. Then use a normal index and drop `lower()`:\n  ```sql\n  CREATE INDEX idx_users_email ON users (email);\n  -- WHERE u.email = 'a@example.org'\n  ```\n  Alternatively, store emails normalized to lowercase at write time and compare directly.\n\n**2. Index the join key.**\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_user_id ON orders (user_id);\n```\nIf you usually sort by recency, `(user_id, created_at)` is better.\n\n**3. Select only the columns you need.**\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.total, u.id AS user_id, u.email\nFROM orders o\nJOIN users u ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n```\nIf the list is small, consider a covering index with `INCLUDE` (Postgres 11+ / SQL Server) on `orders`.\n\n## Expected plan after the fixes\nAn index seek on `users` finds one row. An index seek on `orders.user_id` then finds that user's orders. Cost goes from O(users + orders) to roughly O(log n + matching orders).\n\n## Verify\nI haven't run this. I only had the SQL file, with no schema, row counts or database. Run `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` before and after to confirm the plan changes. Check first whether either index already exists.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}